2026-08-29T07:59Z X2 ran and the follow-up PR is NOT ready to open — the measurement fires the rule the other way. Warm p95 stopword-less = 1 773,0 ms at 477 512 passages against the ~500 ms allowance; a stock-v1.9.0 control arm on the same index and queries answers at 392,3 ms, so the deletion is the cause (4,5x on p95, 5,7x on the median) and DESIGN §3 requires df-driven pruning of >50%-df terms IN this PR, not after it. Artifacts: bench/results/0025-x2-stopwordless/. Second blocker, independent of the number: the PR body's headline claim that 'to be or not to be' tokenizes to nothing is FALSE — tokenize() returns ["not"] on stock, since 'not' was never on the 29-word list, and stock answers the query as a one-term search returning 20 unrelated hits in ~290 ms. Reword before opening. Third blocker, unchanged by any of this: both in-flight upstream slots are spent on #27 and #28
2026-08-29T08:34Z A cheaper fix than the one DESIGN §3 names, worth arbitrating before the PR is rebuilt. The mechanism annex (0025) shows normal queries gain almost nothing from the deletion: 88,1% identical top-20, 19 of 20 same first hit. All the value is in the degenerate query whose tokens are ALL function words. So: keep the stoplist and fall back to the unfiltered token set when filtering leaves too few terms. Normal queries pay nothing and stay at 392,3 ms; the degenerate query is fixed and pays its ~900 ms only when it occurs. df-driven pruning instead spends real complexity making a 5,6x regression smaller, where the fallback never incurs it. Both are language-neutral; the fallback is a far smaller diff to ask the maintainer to take. Author's call — this changes the PR's shape, not just its size
